      <h1>Artifact 4 — Data Challenges in Machine Learning</h1>

      <p class="note">
        This artifact summarizes my responses to a three-part coaching session on core data challenges:
        <strong>missing data</strong>, <strong>noisy data</strong>, and <strong>data drift</strong>.  
        I explained how I would detect each issue, choose a strategy, and balance trade-offs using real examples from my own work.
      </p>

      <h2>Objective</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Show my ability to diagnose and handle common ML data problems.</li>
        <li>Demonstrate structured reasoning instead of just listing techniques.</li>
        <li>Connect decisions to business context and real examples (crypto datasets).</li>
        <li>Highlight how I think about trade-offs like bias, stability, and adaptability.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Scenario 1 — Handling Missing Data</h2>
      <p><strong>Challenge:</strong> Key features in a dataset contain missing values that could distort the model.</p>

      <p><strong>My approach:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Start with EDA and profiling to see <em>where</em> values are missing and <em>why</em> (random gaps, system errors, human input issues, etc.).</li>
        <li>Choose imputation by feature type:
          <ul>
            <li>Numerical → mean, median, or model-based imputation for important fields.</li>
            <li>Categorical → mode or an explicit “Unknown” category.</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li>Remove rows only if too many critical fields are missing and would mislead the model.</li>
        <li>Double-check meaning: sometimes a zero or blank actually has business meaning and should not be treated as an error.</li>
      </ul>

      <p><strong>Example:</strong> In a cryptocurrency OHLCV dataset, I saw missing volume values on certain days.  
      Instead of deleting them immediately, I compared them with market activity and realized the exchange had downtime.  
      That context guided how I filled or handled those records.</p>

      <p><strong>Key insight:</strong> how we treat missing data can introduce bias or hide important patterns, so understanding the cause comes before choosing the method.</p>

      <h2>Scenario 2 — Cleaning Noisy Data</h2>
      <p><strong>Challenge:</strong> Random spikes, extreme values, or measurement errors that hide the true signal.</p>

      <p><strong>My approach:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Use EDA visuals (histograms, boxplots, line charts) to spot unusual spikes or strange shapes in distributions.</li>
        <li>Apply statistical methods such as Z-score or IQR to flag potential outliers.</li>
        <li>Decide based on context:
          <ul>
            <li>If noise comes from bad measurements or system glitches → smooth using rolling averages or similar techniques.</li>
            <li>If “outliers” reflect real rare events → keep them; they may be exactly what the business cares about.</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li>Cap or adjust extreme values when they clearly don’t represent realistic behavior.</li>
      </ul>

      <p><strong>Example:</strong> While analyzing crypto price data, a single exchange sometimes reported a faulty price spike.  
      Using a 7-day rolling average helped smooth those one-off errors while keeping the real trend.</p>

      <p><strong>Trade-off reflection:</strong>  
      Smoothing keeps trends stable but can blur sharp changes.  
      Removing outliers reduces extreme errors but risks losing meaningful rare events.  
      The best choice depends on the story the data is telling and the question the model needs to answer.</p>

      <h2>Scenario 3 — Detecting and Responding to Data Drift</h2>
      <p><strong>Challenge:</strong> A model performs well at first, but accuracy drops as the real-world data distribution changes.</p>

      <p><strong>My approach:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Monitor incoming data distributions versus the original training data.</li>
        <li>Track model metrics (accuracy, F1, etc.) over time to catch early performance drops.</li>
        <li>Use statistical checks (like PSI, KS tests, or simple comparison of means and ranges) to confirm whether drift is happening.</li>
        <li>Retrain the model when needed:
          <ul>
            <li>For fast-moving domains → use a rolling or sliding window of recent data.</li>
            <li>For slower domains → mix recent data with selected historical data.</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p><strong>Example:</strong> In crypto markets, volatility patterns in 2020 and 2024 were very different.  
      A model trained only on 2020 behavior struggled later, so I would retrain using more recent data, with a small amount of history to avoid overreacting to short-term noise.</p>

      <p><strong>My reasoning:</strong> Data drift is normal in production systems.  
      Models are living systems that need checks, monitoring, and retraining, not one-time deployment.</p>

      <h2>Summary Takeaways</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Missing data:</strong> understand patterns first, then choose imputation or removal to avoid bias.</li>
        <li><strong>Noisy data:</strong> detect with visuals and stats; smooth or cap carefully while preserving real signals.</li>
        <li><strong>Data drift:</strong> monitor both data and model performance, detect changes early, and retrain with fresh data.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why This Matters</h2>
      <p>
        These three challenges show up in almost every real ML project.  
        This artifact demonstrates how I think through them step-by-step, using both statistics and domain context.  
        For roles in data analytics, ML engineering, or risk and forecasting, this mindset is essential for keeping models fair, stable, and reliable over time.
      </p>
